  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is the purpose of the CPU?', 'To fetch, decode and execute instructions, processing data to run programs.', 1),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What does the fetch–decode–execute cycle do?', 'The CPU repeatedly fetches an instruction from memory, decodes it to work out what to do, then executes it.', 2),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is the Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU)?', 'The part of the CPU that performs calculations and logical comparisons.', 3),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is the Control Unit (CU)?', 'The part of the CPU that decodes instructions and sends control signals to coordinate the other components.', 4),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is cache?', 'Very fast memory inside or close to the CPU that stores frequently used instructions and data.', 5),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What are registers?', 'Tiny, very fast storage locations inside the CPU that hold data and instructions currently being processed.', 6),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What does the Program Counter (PC) store?', 'The address of the next instruction to be fetched.', 7),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What does the Memory Address Register (MAR) store?', 'The address in memory of the data or instruction being fetched or stored.', 8),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What does the Memory Data Register (MDR) store?', 'The data or instruction that has been fetched from memory or is about to be written to memory.', 9),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What does the accumulator store?', 'The results of calculations carried out by the ALU.', 10),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is Von Neumann architecture?', 'A design where data and instructions are stored together in the same memory, and the CPU processes them one at a time.', 11),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is clock speed?', 'The number of fetch–decode–execute cycles the CPU can carry out per second, measured in hertz (e.g. 3.5 GHz).', 12),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'How does increasing clock speed affect performance?', 'More instructions can be processed per second, so programs run faster.', 13),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is a core?', 'An independent processing unit within the CPU that can fetch, decode and execute instructions.', 14),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'How does having more cores improve performance?', 'Multiple instructions can be processed at the same time, as long as the software is designed to use them.', 15),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'Why might doubling the number of cores not double performance?', 'Some tasks can''t be split up, and cores must communicate and coordinate, which takes time.', 16),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'How does cache size affect performance?', 'A larger cache can store more data close to the CPU, reducing slower accesses to RAM.', 17),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is an embedded system?', 'A computer system built into a larger device to perform a dedicated function, e.g. in a washing machine or car.', 18),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'Give three examples of embedded systems.', 'Washing machines, microwave ovens, car engine management systems, traffic lights and digital watches.', 19),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What are the characteristics of embedded systems?', 'Dedicated function, small size, low power consumption, low cost, and usually reliable.', 20),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is the difference between a general-purpose computer and an embedded system?', 'A general-purpose computer can run many different programs; an embedded system performs one specific task.', 21),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is a bus?', 'A set of wires that carries data, addresses or control signals between components.', 22),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What happens in the fetch stage?', 'The address in the PC is copied to the MAR, the instruction is fetched into the MDR, and the PC is incremented.', 23),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What happens in the decode stage?', 'The control unit works out what the instruction means and what needs to be done.', 24),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What happens in the execute stage?', 'The instruction is carried out, e.g. a calculation in the ALU or data stored in memory.', 25),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'Why is cache faster than RAM?', 'It is physically closer to the CPU and uses faster (more expensive) memory technology.', 26),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is a disadvantage of cache?', 'It is expensive, so only a small amount can be included.', 27),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is the purpose of the address bus?', 'To carry memory addresses from the CPU to memory.', 28),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is the purpose of the data bus?', 'To carry data between the CPU, memory and other components.', 29),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is a GPU?', 'A graphics processing unit, specialised for processing images and parallel tasks.', 30),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is primary storage?', 'Memory that the CPU can access directly, such as RAM and ROM.', 1),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is RAM?', 'Random access memory: volatile memory that stores data, programs and the operating system currently in use.', 2),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What does volatile mean?', 'The contents are lost when the power is turned off.', 3),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is ROM?', 'Read-only memory: non-volatile memory that stores the boot-up instructions (BIOS).', 4),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is the difference between RAM and ROM?', 'RAM is volatile and can be written to; ROM is non-volatile and usually read-only.', 5),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'Why do computers need primary storage?', 'To hold data and instructions the CPU needs quickly, because secondary storage is too slow.', 6),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is virtual memory?', 'Part of secondary storage used as extra RAM when RAM is full.', 7),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'Why is virtual memory slow?', 'Secondary storage is much slower than RAM, so swapping data in and out slows the computer down.', 8),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is secondary storage?', 'Non-volatile storage used to keep data permanently, e.g. hard drives and SSDs.', 9),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'Why do computers need secondary storage?', 'To store files, programs and the operating system permanently when the power is off.', 10),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is magnetic storage?', 'Storage that uses magnetised regions on spinning platters, e.g. a hard disk drive (HDD).', 11),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is optical storage?', 'Storage that uses lasers to read and write data on discs, e.g. CDs, DVDs and Blu-ray.', 12),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is solid-state storage?', 'Storage with no moving parts that uses flash memory, e.g. SSDs and USB drives.', 13),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'Give an advantage and disadvantage of magnetic storage.', 'Advantage: high capacity at low cost. Disadvantage: moving parts can be damaged, and it is slower than SSD.', 14),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'Give an advantage and disadvantage of solid-state storage.', 'Advantage: fast, durable and portable. Disadvantage: more expensive per GB.', 15),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'Give an advantage and disadvantage of optical storage.', 'Advantage: cheap and portable. Disadvantage: low capacity and easily scratched.', 16),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What are the characteristics used to compare storage?', 'Capacity, speed, portability, durability, reliability and cost.', 17),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is a bit?', 'A binary digit: 0 or 1, the smallest unit of data.', 18),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is a nibble?', '4 bits.', 19),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is a byte?', '8 bits.', 20),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What are the units of data from smallest to largest?', 'Bit, nibble, byte, kilobyte (1000 bytes), megabyte, gigabyte, terabyte, petabyte.', 21),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'Why do computers use binary?', 'Computers are made of switches (transistors) that have two states: on and off.', 22),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'Convert binary 1011 to denary.', '8 + 0 + 2 + 1 = 11.', 23),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'Convert denary 45 to 8-bit binary.', '00101101.', 24),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'Convert hexadecimal 2F to denary.', '2 × 16 + 15 = 47.', 25),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'Why is hexadecimal used?', 'It is shorter and easier for humans to read and write than binary, with fewer errors.', 26),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is a character set?', 'A defined list of characters and the binary codes that represent them, e.g. ASCII or Unicode.', 27),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'How is an image stored?', 'As a grid of pixels, each represented by a binary number for its colour.', 28),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is colour depth?', 'The number of bits used to represent the colour of each pixel.', 29),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'How is sound stored digitally?', 'The amplitude of the sound wave is sampled at regular intervals and each sample is stored as a binary number.', 30),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is a network?', 'Two or more computers connected together to share data and resources.', 1),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is a LAN?', 'A local area network covering a small geographical area, such as a school, using its own hardware.', 2),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is a WAN?', 'A wide area network covering a large geographical area, often using third-party infrastructure; the internet is the largest WAN.', 3),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What factors affect network performance?', 'Bandwidth, number of users, transmission media, errors, and interference.', 4),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is bandwidth?', 'The amount of data that can be transmitted per second.', 5),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is a client–server network?', 'A network where servers provide services and resources to client computers.', 6),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is a peer-to-peer network?', 'A network where all computers have equal status and share resources directly with each other.', 7),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'Give an advantage of a client–server network.', 'Centralised security, backups and file management.', 8),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'Give an advantage of a peer-to-peer network.', 'Cheap and easy to set up, with no dependence on a server.', 9),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is a wireless access point (WAP)?', 'A device that allows wireless devices to connect to a wired network.', 10),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is a router?', 'A device that forwards data packets between networks, e.g. between a LAN and the internet.', 11),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is a switch?', 'A device that connects devices on a LAN and sends data only to the intended device using MAC addresses.', 12),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is a NIC?', 'A network interface card (controller), which allows a device to connect to a network.', 13),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is the internet?', 'A worldwide collection of interconnected networks.', 14),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is DNS?', 'The Domain Name System, which translates domain names (e.g. bbc.co.uk) into IP addresses.', 15),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is hosting?', 'Storing websites or files on a server connected to the internet so others can access them.', 16),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is the cloud?', 'Remote servers accessed over the internet to store data and run software.', 17),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'Give an advantage and disadvantage of the cloud.', 'Advantage: access from anywhere, no need for local storage. Disadvantage: needs internet access and raises security concerns.', 18),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is a star topology?', 'All devices connect to a central switch.', 19),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is a mesh topology?', 'Devices are connected to many other devices, so data has several possible routes.', 20),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'Give an advantage of a star topology.', 'If one cable fails, the other devices are unaffected, and it is easy to add devices.', 21),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'Give an advantage of a mesh topology.', 'No single point of failure; data can take another route if a connection fails.', 22),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is Wi-Fi?', 'A wireless networking standard using radio waves to connect devices to a network.', 23),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is Ethernet?', 'A wired networking standard for connecting devices in a LAN.', 24),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is an IP address?', 'A unique address given to each device on a network, used to route data.', 25),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is a MAC address?', 'A unique, permanent hardware address assigned to a network interface.', 26),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is a protocol?', 'A set of rules for how data is transmitted across a network.', 27),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What are TCP/IP and HTTP used for?', 'TCP/IP: sending data across the internet in packets. HTTP: requesting and transferring web pages.', 28),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is HTTPS?', 'A secure version of HTTP that encrypts data between browser and server.', 29),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.3', 'Networks & Protocols', 'What is the concept of layers in networking?', 'Protocols are divided into layers, each handling a specific part of communication, making development and troubleshooting easier.', 30),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is malware?', 'Malicious software designed to damage, disrupt or gain unauthorised access to computer systems.', 1),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is a virus?', 'Malware that attaches itself to files and spreads when those files are copied or opened.', 2),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is a worm?', 'Malware that replicates itself and spreads across networks without user action.', 3),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is a Trojan?', 'Malware disguised as legitimate software.', 4),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is ransomware?', 'Malware that encrypts a user''s files and demands payment for their release.', 5),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is spyware?', 'Malware that secretly monitors user activity and collects information.', 6),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is social engineering?', 'Manipulating people into giving away confidential information.', 7),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is phishing?', 'Sending fake emails or messages that appear to be from a trusted source to trick people into revealing personal information.', 8),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is shouldering?', 'Watching someone enter a password or PIN over their shoulder.', 9),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is a brute-force attack?', 'Trying every possible combination of characters to guess a password.', 10),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is a denial of service (DoS) attack?', 'Flooding a server with requests so it can''t respond to genuine users.', 11),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is data interception?', 'Capturing data as it travels across a network, e.g. using packet sniffers.', 12),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is SQL injection?', 'Entering malicious SQL code into an input field to access or change a database.', 13),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is penetration testing?', 'Authorised simulated attacks on a system to find vulnerabilities.', 14),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is anti-malware software?', 'Software that detects, prevents and removes malware.', 15),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is a firewall?', 'Hardware or software that monitors network traffic and blocks unauthorised access.', 16),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What are user access levels?', 'Permissions that control which files and resources different users can access.', 17),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'Why are strong passwords important?', 'They are harder to guess or brute-force, protecting accounts from unauthorised access.', 18),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What makes a strong password?', 'A long password mixing upper and lower case letters, numbers and symbols, not based on personal information.', 19),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is encryption?', 'Scrambling data using a key so it can only be read by someone with the correct key.', 20),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is physical security?', 'Protecting hardware from theft or damage, e.g. locks, CCTV and security guards.', 21),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is two-factor authentication?', 'Requiring two forms of verification, such as a password and a code sent to a phone.', 22),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'How can SQL injection be prevented?', 'Validating input and using parameterised queries.', 23),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'How can phishing be prevented?', 'User training, spam filters, and checking email addresses and links before clicking.', 24),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'How can brute-force attacks be prevented?', 'Strong passwords, account lockouts after failed attempts, and CAPTCHAs.', 25),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'How can DoS attacks be reduced?', 'Firewalls, traffic filtering, and increasing server capacity.', 26),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is a vulnerability?', 'A weakness in a system that can be exploited by an attacker.', 27),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'Why do organisations need network policies?', 'To define acceptable use, security procedures and responsibilities, reducing risks.', 28),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is a backup?', 'A copy of data stored separately so it can be restored if the original is lost or damaged.', 29),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.4', 'Network Security', 'What is the difference between hackers and malware?', 'Hackers are people who gain unauthorised access; malware is software used to cause harm.', 30),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is an operating system?', 'System software that manages hardware and software and provides a user interface.', 1),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is a user interface?', 'The means by which a user interacts with a computer, e.g. a GUI or command line.', 2),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is a GUI?', 'A graphical user interface using windows, icons, menus and pointers.', 3),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is a command-line interface?', 'A text-based interface where the user types commands.', 4),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is memory management?', 'The OS allocates memory to programs, moves data between RAM and virtual memory, and prevents programs interfering with each other.', 5),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is multitasking?', 'Running more than one program at the same time, with the OS sharing CPU time between them.', 6),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is peripheral management?', 'The OS communicates with input and output devices using device drivers.', 7),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is a device driver?', 'Software that allows the OS to communicate with a hardware device.', 8),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is user management?', 'The OS controls user accounts, logins, passwords and access rights.', 9),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is file management?', 'The OS organises files into folders, controls naming, and allows files to be moved, copied and deleted.', 10),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is utility software?', 'Software that helps maintain and optimise a computer, e.g. antivirus, backup and compression.', 11),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is defragmentation?', 'Reorganising files on a hard disk so their parts are stored together, improving access speed.', 12),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'Why isn''t defragmentation needed on an SSD?', 'SSDs have no moving parts, so data access time isn''t affected by fragmentation; it would also cause unnecessary wear.', 13),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is compression software?', 'Software that reduces file size to save storage space and speed up transfer.', 14),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is encryption software?', 'Software that encrypts data so it can''t be read without a key.', 15),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is a full backup?', 'A copy of all data.', 16),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is an incremental backup?', 'A backup of only the data that has changed since the last backup.', 17),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'Give an advantage of incremental backups.', 'They are faster and use less storage space than full backups.', 18),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'Give a disadvantage of incremental backups.', 'Restoring data takes longer because the full backup and all increments are needed.', 19),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is system software?', 'Software that manages the computer, including the operating system and utilities.', 20),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is application software?', 'Software that helps users perform tasks, e.g. word processors and web browsers.', 21),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'Give three examples of operating systems.', 'Windows, macOS, Linux, Android and iOS.', 22),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'Why does an operating system need to manage the CPU?', 'To schedule processes so multiple programs can share CPU time efficiently.', 23),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is paging?', 'Dividing memory into fixed-size blocks so data can be swapped between RAM and virtual memory.', 24),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What happens when a computer boots up?', 'The BIOS in ROM runs self-tests and loads the operating system from secondary storage into RAM.', 25),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is anti-virus software?', 'Utility software that scans files for known malware signatures and removes or quarantines threats.', 26),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is the role of a file manager?', 'Allowing users to organise and navigate files and folders.', 27),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'What is a disk cleanup utility?', 'Software that removes unnecessary files to free up storage space.', 28),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.5', 'Systems Software', 'Why does the OS need to control user access?', 'To keep data secure and ensure users can only access what they are permitted to.', 29),
